.TH IP\-IOAM 8 "05 Jul 2021" "iproute2" "Linux"
.SH "NAME"
ip-ioam \- IPv6 In-situ OAM (IOAM)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.sp
.ad l
.in +8
.ti -8
.B ip ioam
.RI " { " COMMAND " | "
.BR help " }"
.sp
.ti -8

.ti -8
.B ip ioam namespace show

.ti -8
.B ip ioam namespace add
.I ID
.BR " [ "
.B data
.I DATA32
.BR "]"
.BR " [ "
.B wide
.I DATA64
.BR "]"

.ti -8
.B ip ioam namespace del
.I ID

.ti -8
.B ip ioam schema show

.ti -8
.B ip ioam schema add
.I ID DATA

.ti -8
.B ip ioam schema del
.I ID

.ti -8
.B ip ioam gobschema show

.ti -8
.B ip ioam gobschema add
.I ID
.B object
.I PATH
.B section
.I SECTION_NAME

.ti -8
.B ip ioam gobschema del
.I ID

.ti -8
.B ip ioam namespace set
.I ID
.B schema
.RI " { " ID " | "
.BR none " }"

.ti -8
.B ip ioam namespace set
.I ID
.B gobschema
.RI " { " ID " | "
.BR none " } [ "
.B gobimplied
.IR SIZE " ]"

.ti -8
.B ip ioam monitor

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBip ioam\fR command is used to configure IPv6 In-situ OAM (IOAM6)
internal parameters, namely IOAM namespaces and schemas.
.PP
Those parameters also include the mapping between an IOAM namespace and an IOAM
schema.
.PP
The \fBip ioam monitor\fR command displays IOAM data received.
.PP
The \fBgobschema\fR commands configure the processing of the Global Opaque
Block (GOB), a block of opaque data shared by all the nodes on the path and
carried at the tail of the IOAM Pre-allocated Trace Option
(draft-mayer-bpm-ioam-gob). A GOB schema binds a numerical \fIID\fR to an eBPF
program of type \fBBPF_PROG_TYPE_IOAM6_GOB\fR, loaded from the section
\fISECTION_NAME\fR of the ELF object file \fIPATH\fR. The program runs on the
GOB payload of the packets of every IOAM namespace the GOB schema is linked to.
.PP
An IOAM namespace handles the GOB in one of two modes:
.TP
default mode
\fBnamespace set\fR \fIID\fR \fBgobschema\fR \fIID\fR, without
\fBgobimplied\fR. The GOB is present only in the packets that have the G bit
set in the trace header. Its size and its schema are read from the GOB trailer,
the last 4 octets of the option. The program runs when the schema in the
trailer matches the \fIID\fR of the GOB schema linked to the namespace.
.TP
namespace-implied mode
\fBnamespace set\fR \fIID\fR \fBgobschema\fR { \fIID\fR | \fBnone\fR }
\fBgobimplied\fR \fISIZE\fR. Every packet of the namespace carries a GOB of
\fISIZE\fR octets at the tail of the option, with no GOB trailer; the G bit is
not used. \fISIZE\fR must be a non-zero multiple of 4, at most 240. With
\fBgobschema none\fR the node knows the size of the GOB but runs no program
on it.
.PP
The GOB schema and the implied size are always set together: a
\fBnamespace set\fR ... \fBgobschema\fR command without \fBgobimplied\fR
puts the namespace back in the default mode.

.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.SS Configure an IOAM namespace (ID = 1) with both data (32 bits) and wide data (64 bits)
.nf
# ip ioam namespace add 1 data 0xdeadbeef wide 0xcafec0caf00dc0de
.PP
.SS Link an existing IOAM schema (ID = 7) to an existing IOAM namespace (ID = 1)
.nf
# ip ioam namespace set 1 schema 7
.PP
.SS Load an eBPF program as GOB schema (ID = 170) and link it to an existing IOAM namespace (ID = 1) in the default mode
.nf
# ip ioam gobschema add 170 object gob.bpf.o section ioam6_gob
# ip ioam namespace set 1 gobschema 170
.PP
.SS Link the same GOB schema to the IOAM namespace in the namespace-implied mode, with a GOB of 16 octets
.nf
# ip ioam namespace set 1 gobschema 170 gobimplied 16
.SH SEE ALSO
.br
.BR ip-route (8)
